# Jenkins CI often executes from `main`

Many Jenkins multibranch / Pipeline-from-SCM setups load the `Jenkinsfile`
(or pipeline entrypoint) from `main` (or another tracked default branch), or
from the branch under build only after it is pushed. Working-tree-only edits
have **no effect** until SCM has the commit the job checks out.

## Rule

When you change a `Jenkinsfile` or pipeline entrypoint:

1. Follow `jenkins-pipeline` (and `code-yaml` when the entrypoint is YAML).
2. Commit related files together (pipeline plus scripts it calls) when the
   user asks to commit.
3. Remind the user that the job must see the commit on the branch it builds
   (often default for some folder jobs; head branch for multibranch PR jobs).
4. Do **not** commit or push automatically unless the consuming project has
   an explicit preference for that.

## Why

SCM-driven Jenkins checks out a specific ref. A pipeline change is “live”
only for builds that check out a commit containing it. Verify with
`jenkins-builds` after a run that could see the new file.
